# Must sit at the ROOT of the build context, not next to the Dockerfile. Docker
# only reads the one at the context root (or <dockerfile>.dockerignore), and a
# misplaced copy is silently ignored -- which is how a 654 MB context and a
# clobbered obj/project.assets.json get through.
#
# The context is the repository root so that both images (agent and data API)
# can be built from it with one set of rules.

**/.git
**/.vs
**/bin
**/obj
**/node_modules
**/ClientApp/node_modules
**/.venv
**/__pycache__
**/logs
**/*.sqlite
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.sqlite-shm
**/*.sqlite-wal
**/checkpoints.sqlite*

# .env holds the LongCat key. It must be supplied at run time as an environment
# variable or a Docker secret, never baked into an image layer -- a layer is
# readable by anyone who can pull the image, even if a later layer "deletes" it.
**/.env

# Development settings never reach a runtime image. The csprojs already strip
# them from publish, and the Dockerfiles set ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production.
**/appsettings.Development.json

# Not needed to build either image. Excluded to keep the context small.
src/OpsLookup.Graph/
docs/
tools/
*.md